From b9e89296755d6bc55fee3f0fc866feb2f8c4dfc4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 01:10:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] forms: interactive range/number/list box widgets and form
 pseudo-class states

<input type=range> had no interaction code: clicks, drags and keys did nothing.
Pressing the primary button now moves the thumb under the pointer and starts a
drag that follows it until release, firing input on each value change and change
on release. The drag uses a new widget mouse capture on the document event
handler (like Chrome's mouse-capturing node) so it keeps tracking outside the
slider without firing gotpointercapture. Arrow keys, Page Up/Down, Home and End
move the value with Chrome's step and page-step rules and fire input + change.

<input type=number> now steps with ArrowUp/ArrowDown using stepUp/stepDown
semantics (min/max/step; step=any uses the default step, as Chrome does) and has
a ::-webkit-inner-spin-button in its UA shadow tree that shows on hover and steps
the value when either half is pressed. A change fired this way is recorded as
committed so blurring the field does not fire a second one.

<select multiple> and <select size>1> rendered as a drop-down reading "N
selected". They now render as a list box: the options and optgroups are slotted
into a scroller that is display-size rows tall (or fills an author-sized
<select>), selected rows are highlighted through option:checked (inactive grey,
focused blue, as in Chrome), and click / Ctrl-click / Shift-click plus the arrow
keys, Home and End change the selection and send select update notifications.
List boxes no longer open the embedder picker; drop-down selects keep using it.

ElementState flags that were never set are now maintained:
:user-valid/:user-invalid from a user validity flag on ValidityState, set when
the user commits a change (text controls on blur/Enter, checkbox, radio, file,
select, range, number) and on form submission, cleared on reset;
:in-range/:out-of-range for inputs with range limitations; :default for
checked-by-default checkboxes and radios, options with a selected attribute and
the form's default button (also matching button:default); and :checked for
selected options.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
